Well tests, intakes and observations that constrain a source (@wu-source-observations: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #water-and-sanitation "Source-contamination-pathways-and-protection.lingua#r_6ZM37SHMBFXZRZ0XK2PWWS5E5R") { r_33V2BHHRAESJC6434PABCV64PD
**Read:** [Manual de Saneamento](../sources/Manual-de-Saneamento-Funasa.lingua#r_65G5YY3QVCA8HAXMPC8RTPKEWE), abstraction and source material; [Groundwater Resource Development](../sources/Groundwater-resource-development.lingua#r_1M3NS4Y0CV4391JF136S20MVBP), chapter 4's estimation methods and chapter 6's cases. Revisit the groundwater flow and transport Records linked from this trail's geology material. Distinguish a well's mechanical delivery capacity from a water body's ability to supply it without unacceptable consequences.

A time series of pumping rate and water level supports a different inference from a single static depth. Record the datum, well geometry, screen intervals, measurement times, other pumping and recovery period. Drawdown is change relative to a stated reference, not depth below an arbitrary ruler. Specific capacity Q/drawdown is an observed ratio with a test duration; it is not automatically aquifer transmissivity. Well losses, partial penetration, leakage and boundaries can produce alternative interpretations.

For an intake, record water levels and quality at candidate depths, access during floods and droughts, sediment and debris, approach conditions, interruption procedures and maintenance access. An apparent short-term intake capacity says little about dry-season availability. A second intake can share the same catchment failure; two pumps can share one power supply.

**Work:** Design an observation programme, not a real drilling or abstraction operation. Supply a hypothetical pumping schedule and identify the observations needed to test a model. Hold back part of a recovery series and ask whether a model fitted to pumping predicts it. State which parameters are non-identifiable and which extra observation could help. Compare surface intake and well options using availability, quality, energy, maintainability and uncertainty, with each conclusion tied to an observation or explicit assumption.

**Continue:** [Source contamination pathways and protection](Source-contamination-pathways-and-protection.lingua#r_6ZM37SHMBFXZRZ0XK2PWWS5E5R).
